\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Commissioner Mollica read the recognition into the record.\par
\par
John Miller and Kaylee Howell, Historical Society Archivist, presented a brief history of the Colony Hotel, noting it was incorporated in 1924 by Alfred T. Rapp during Delray Beach's building boom. They highlighted original stock certificates, newspaper articles, and historic images documenting the hotel's construction, designed by renowned architect Martin Luther Hampton. Ms. Howell explained the hotel featured modern innovations for the era, including fireproof construction, automatic sprinklers, a ladies' parlor, and writing room, making it a premier resort. The hotel officially opened on March 1, 1926, during Prohibition, with a grand opening dinner costing $2.\par
\par
Ms. Howell noted the 1926 hurricanes ended the land boom, leading to financial difficulties for Alfred T. Rapp and the hotel, which was later listed for sale. In 1935, George, Agnes, and Charles Boughton purchased and revitalized the property, creating the Colony Hotel known today. Mr. Miller and Ms. Howell shared historic postcards and photographs illustrating the Colony's role as the center of civic life, hosting meetings, gatherings, social events, and serving as a hurricane shelter, including during the 1949 hurricane. Mr. Miller concluded that the Boughton family continues to own the hotel, recognizing it as one of South Florida's most important examples of historic preservation and a member of the National Trust for Historic Preservation and Historic Hotels of America, wishing the Colony Hotel a happy centennial.\par
\par
Mayor Carney thanked the presenters, stating the Colony Hotel is a great part of the city and has been an important landmark for many years. Commissioner Casale thanked the presenters, recognized the Colony Hotel as an outstanding example of historic preservation, and expressed appreciation to Archivist Kaylee Howell for preserving Delray Beach's history for future generations.\par
}

