\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Janai Bowens, Assistant Neighborhood & Community Services Director/Education Liaison, introduced Laurie Sallarulo, representing Junior Achievement of South Florida, to provide an overview of its programs and resources.\par
\par
Ms. Sallarulo discussed the issue of workforce development and the need to expose students to a broad range of career opportunities beginning in elementary school. She explained that JA BizTown begins in fifth grade and provides classroom lessons followed by a full-day simulation where students learn about careers, businesses, money management, entrepreneurship, and work skills. She stated that eighth-grade students participate in Career Discovery Park, which provides deeper career exploration and develops communication, problem-solving, critical-thinking, and other essential work skills. Ms. Sallarulo stated that Junior Achievement works with every elementary and middle school in Broward County and also in Boca Raton. She explained that the organization requested $25,000 per school to serve two Delray Beach schools for a three-year period, with training for teachers, classroom support, and transportation to JA World included. She emphasized that the programs connect education to the real world and employment. Commissioner Casale clarified that the request was $50,000 per year for two schools for three years. Ms. Sallarulo confirmed the amount. The schools identified were Village Academy for JA BizTown and Carver Middle School for Career Discovery Park. Vice Mayor Burns thanked Junior Achievement for the presentation and stated that she was familiar with the organization from her experience in education. She noted that Junior Achievement has an established relationship with the school district and that its programs provide a needed way for the City to support students in Delray Beach schools.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Ms. Bowens stated that the presentation was intended to clarify the City\rquote s purchasing and procurement strategies related to the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD), current solicitation processes in Neighborhood and Community Services, and compliance with the Uniform Federal Grant Guidance.\par
\par
Ms. Bowens explained that the Department oversees the Community Development Block Grant (CDBG), State Housing Initiatives Partnership (SHIP), CRA-funded curb appeal grants, and the Urban Development Action Grant (UDAG). She stated that the grants are governed by HUD, State of Florida, and City purchasing and procurement guidance, requiring compliance with conflict-of-interest standards, free and open competition, proper documentation, transparent communication, and publicly available evaluation criteria. She outlined the six phases of the federal grant procurement process: Phase One is the needs assessment; Phase Two is development of the Notice of Funding or Request for Proposals; Phase Three is the public solicitation and outreach; Phase Four is receipt of applications; Phase Five is Commission review and decision; and Phase Six is the award and closeout.\par
\par
Ms. Bowens explained that the cone of silence begins when the solicitation is released to the public and applies to staff, representatives involved in the solicitation, Commissioners and their staff, and employees authorized to act on behalf of the City. She stated that the cone of silence protects the integrity, fairness, and transparency of the process by ensuring all vendors have access to the same information. Ms. Bowens stated that questions and clarification during the solicitation process must be submitted through BidNet Direct and answered by the Purchasing and Procurement Department to maintain a transparent record for HUD and State audits. Permitted activities include procedural questions through Procurement, reviewing publicly available solicitation documents, attending publicly noticed meetings, submitting written questions before deadlines, and requesting information through official addenda. Prohibited activities include discussing proposal rankings or evaluations, contacting evaluation committee members, attempting to influence City staff, and requesting confidential scoring information.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Sam Metott, Parks & Recreation Director provided an update on the Delray Beach Municipal Cemetery, noting that the cemetery was incorporated in 1903 and has a significant history of maintenance issues. He stated that in 2018, the City hired a full-time cemetery manager to improve the reservation and burial process and reduce errors. The City also implemented a respectful cleanup initiative, improved access and staff coverage, contracted weekly maintenance, installed new perimeter fencing, expanded and replotted the cemetery, enhanced landscaping, and began painting above-ground tombs every two years.\par
\par
Mr. Metott stated that the City is planning two new mausoleum buildings that would add more than 2,000 tombs and niches. He noted that the mausoleums would provide additional capacity but would be more expensive than traditional burial and would not meet every family\rquote s needs. He stated that future discussions would address continuous maintenance, cemetery improvements, and whether the City should acquire additional land, close the cemetery to new burials while maintaining it respectfully, or identify other options. Mr. Metott stated that a part-time employee would be cross-trained to provide cemetery coverage on Mondays. He also stated that the City received quotes from a maintenance company to conduct an entire sweep of the cemetery, including cleaning headstones and trimming vegetation around them.\par
\par
Vice Mayor Burns asked whether the maintenance company was specialized in historic cemetery maintenance. Mr. Metott stated that the company was provided the guidance from the historic report so it understood the required maintenance standards. He further stated that there are currently no in-ground plots available for new purchase and that the City can only accommodate residents who previously purchased plots through pre-need. He clarified that the rule limiting new plot purchases to Delray Beach residents remains in effect.\par
\par
Commissioner Casale asked for clarification regarding the photographs in the presentation and whether the shade trees shown would be removed. Mr. Metott confirmed that the shade trees would remain intact.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mr. Moore explained that Zambelli Fireworks International notified the City on July 1 that the barge scheduled for the July 4 offshore fireworks display was unavailable. Staff from Parks and Recreation, Public Works, Fire Rescue, and Police worked to implement a contingency plan, resulting in a close-proximity fireworks display launched from the beach. Mr. Moore stated that the City was treated poorly and disrespectfully by Zambelli Fireworks International and commended staff and leadership for their efforts.\par
\par
Mayor Carney, Commissioner Casale, and Deputy Vice Mayor Markert questioned the City\rquote s recourse against Zambelli and whether the City would be reimbursed. Ms.Gelin stated that the City\rquote s contract is with Zambelli, not its subcontractor, and that the contract contains no penalties, fines, or reimbursement provisions for nonperformance. She stated that the City\rquote s recourse was to cancel the contract and that she would review the matter and report back to the Commission. Deputy Vice Mayor Markert stated that the City should pursue the matter because Zambelli failed to deliver and the City incurred damages from public expectations and advertising. Mayor Carney stated that future contracts should include penalty clauses. Ms. Gelin agreed that future contracts should include a penalty clause and stated that she would look into the matter.\par
\par
Deputy Vice Mayor Markert recommended considering alternative venues for future fireworks because the City had experienced the barge issue two years in a row. He suggested locations such as Old School Square and other land-based venues and requested that options be brought back to the public. Mr. Moore stated that he had already committed to working with staff and departments to consider alternatives in advance of 2027. Mayor Carney suggested drone shows, while Commissioner Casale noted that they are expensive during the Fourth of July period.\par
}

