\viewkind4\uc1\pard\kerning2\f0\fs24 Ms. Gelin read Ordinance No. 11-26 into the record. \par
\par
Ms. Gianniotes explained that the proposed adjustment was Commission-directed and addressed the more intense pet uses, including boarding and other services. She stated that the City had revised pet service regulations in 2021 after several businesses caused neighborhood disruptions, resulting in the Commission requiring conditional use review for pet services. Since then, the City has gradually rolled back those requirements, including allowing veterinary offices as permitted use because they are well regulated by the State and no conditional use request has been denied. Ms. Gianniotes explained that pet grooming businesses are generally small businesses but are currently subject to the conditional use process, which is costly and takes several months. Four businesses have gone through the process and been approved. She stated that the proposed amendment would allow standalone pet grooming and similar small-scale uses to be approved administratively through the Certificate of Use process, provided zoning and interior buildouts confirm there is no play area or outside use area. Pet hotels, animal shelters, and doggy daycares where animals may be present overnight or may create barking impacts would remain conditional uses. In industrial districts, more intense uses such as training, boarding, pet hotels, and shelters would be allowed without conditional use review, while grooming would only be permitted as an accessory use to those more intense uses. Ms. Gianniotes stated that the proposal was a \ldblquote business-friendly option\rdblquote\b \b0 and represented the right balance of allowing small grooming businesses as permitted uses throughout commercial districts while allowing more intense uses in industrial districts without conditional use review. The Planning and Zoning Board reviewed the amendment at its May meeting and recommended approval 6-0.\par
\par
Mayor Carney opened the floor to anyone who wished to speak on his item. Seeing no present, Mayor Carney closed public comment.\cf1\kerning0\f1\par
