{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Ms. Brandon read Ordinance No. 31-26 into the record.\par
\par
Anthea Gianniotes, Development Services Director, presented the first of three city-initiated amendments, which seeks to clarify how alleys are treated for setback purposes. The Central Business District (CBD) treats alleys as rear property lines, but the code is silent on this issue outside the CBD, resulting in differing interpretations and recent application of the side street setback. The proposed amendment would clarify that side lot lines adjoining alleys are regulated by rear setbacks, except in the R1AB district and where additional setback requirements apply. The Planning and Zoning Board recommended approval, 6-0, on June 15.\par
\par
Mayor Carney opened the floor to public comment. Seeing no one present. Mayor Carney closed public comment.\par
\par
Commissioner Casale asked whether the amendment would conflict with future accessory dwelling unit requirements. Ms. Gianniotes explained that the amendment clarifies alley setbacks, with staff recommending rear setbacks to avoid onerous requirements and provide adequate space.\par
}

