\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Henry Dachowitz, Chief Financial Officer, discussed the monthly budget amendments as follows:\par
\par
Parks and Recreation: $250,000 revenue increase from prior year surplus and $250,000 expenditure increase to other current charges for hosting the Billie Jean King tournament; amounts balance in the General Fund.\par
\par
Development Services: $8,812.54 transfer from freight and postage to rental and lease equipment for Crywolf, plus $37 transfer from office supplies to tuition reimbursement; total: $8,849.54.\par
\par
Water and Sewer Fund: $8,000 transfer from general operating supplies to service equipment maintenance for camera repair; $62,465 transfer from salary wages and $300,000 from non-operating contingency to various general ledger accounts for billing, incentive pay, overtime, engineering changes, upgrades, and reclassification; total: $370,465.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Laura Simon, Director of the Downtown Development Authority (DDA), acknowledged the DDA board members in attendance. She noted that the DDA was established in 1971 and expanded in 1993 and 1998 to its current district. She stated that the one-mill assessment was not being raised and would fund the DDA\rquote s operations. She discussed the DDA's priorities, programs and accomplishments. She added that the city will be celebrating its 100th anniversary and that the DDA would work with City partners and the beautification task force to outline what downtown would look like for the next 100 years.\par
\par
She stated Old School Square remained a priority and that venue rentals had grown significantly, requiring continued operations, manpower, marketing, and branding. She noted the DDA\rquote s primary funding was $800,000, with additional revenue from venue rentals, sponsorships, and program income. She requested that the City adopt the one-mill rate, with a small increase to refund back into downtown and reinvest back into the community.\par
\par
Mayor Carney opened the floor to public comments. Seeing none, public comments were closed.\par
}

