\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Carney read the proclamation into the record. \par
\par
Elizabeth Burrows, Director of Development and Community Relations for 211 Palm Beach and Treasure Coast, spoke about suicide prevention and its awareness. 211 provides support 24/7 through phone calls, text messages, online chats, and emails. She encouraged everyone to remember 988 and 211, noting that services are confidential, free, and available to everyone. She thanked the City Commission for its support.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Sergio Piedra, Associate Vice President of Intergovernmental Affairs for Discover the Palm Beaches, introduced the organization and discussed its tourism promotion, marketing, and community involvement. He stated that Delray Beach is vital to the organization\rquote s story and highlighted its community assets and destination stewardship initiatives.\par
\par
Gustav Weibull, Senior Vice President of Destination Technology and Intelligence for Discover the Palm Beaches, reported that Delray Beach welcomed 593,000 visitors in 2025, up 4% from 2024. The city has nearly 1,400 hotel rooms, with occupancy reaching almost 72% and room rates increasing by $7. Resident sentiment toward tourism also improved in 2026, reflecting greater recognition of its economic value. Upcoming Class A office developments are expected to generate 19,000 additional hotel room nights and increase occupancy by 3.5%, benefiting existing hotels, as no new hotels are currently in the pipeline. Mr. Weibull highlighted Destination Demand, a proprietary tool recently granted a patent, that forecasts demand weekly for the next six months and identifies soft periods. Implemented in 2023, it helped grow market shares and Florida hotel rooms revenue from 5% to 5.7% by targeting the right audiences at the right time, while other destinations experienced declines.\par
\par
Milton Segarra stated that Discover the Palm Beaches strengthens the visitor economy through brand stewardship and storytelling. He explained that tourism is funded by the 6% room tax paid by visitors, not residents. Each marketing dollar generates $3.55 in bed taxes, $133 in visitor spending, $9.31 in state and local taxes, and $193 in economic impact. He encouraged developing a shared brand element without changing existing logos.\par
\par
Mayor Carney commended the gentlemen for their presentation and bringing film crews, visitors, and revenue to Palm Beach County and Delray Beach.\par
}

