{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Duncan Webb presented the Old School Square needs assessment and market study, outlining the campus\rquote market potential, competing facilities, and opportunities for local and regional arts programming. He recommended strengthening Old School Square as Delray Beach\rquote s town square, expanding arts education across the campus, and building the capacity to become the best place to put on events in Delray Beach. He discussed potential uses for the Crest Theater, Creative Arts School, Cornell Museum, Field House, and amphitheater, emphasizing the need to balance events with neighborhood impacts. The next phase will focus on programming, governance, operations, financial sustainability, and determining how to manage the distinct needs of each facility while maintaining a unified campus-wide vision.\par
\par
\cf0\kerning2\f1 Commissioner Casale expressed disappointment that the presentation largely repeated information the Commission already knew and emphasized the need for a long-term operational plan outlining viable options for managing the campus and its individual components. \cf1\kerning0\f0 Additionally, she asked how the tourism plan would factor into this. Mr. Webb stated that there are opportunities for Old School Square to support and drive tourism, particularly as it relates to campus-wide events, fairs, and festivals. The second phase would develop a business plan addressing programming, operations, and financial sustainability. \cf0\kerning2\f1 He estimated that the work would take three months after authorization to proceed. \cf1\kerning0\f0 Missie Barletto, Public Works Director, explained that the second phase would require a separate contract and a cost of $50,000 per phase. They would proceed if the Commission agreed with the proposed vision.\par
\par
\cf0\kerning2\f1 Vice Mayor Burns supported giving serious consideration to the Arts Garage partnership proposal, and expressed disappointment that additional funding would be required for the second phase. She emphasized the need to move forward with reopening and activating the facility, noting that it had been dark for five or six years. \cf1\kerning0\f0 Deputy Vice Mayor Markert supported continuing the study to identify operational options and recommendations. Commissioner Mollica stated that coordinating five separate operators would be impractical. Mayor Carney expressed the need to move forward expeditiously with a plan the City could adopt.\par
}

