\viewkind4\uc1\pard\fs24 Commissioner Mollica requested that Item No. 6.C. be pulled for discussion. It became Item No. 7.A.A. on the Regular Agenda.\par
\par
\kerning2\f1 Commissioner Mollica questioned requiring property owners to dedicate land for roadway and alley widths before receiving construction permits. Ms. Gelin explained that dedications are typically requested during development when the Comprehensive Plan identifies future roadway widening, as later dedications could affect existing structures and setbacks. Ms. Gelin confirmed that this typically applies to new development.\par
\par
Missie Barletto, Public Works Director, explained that right-of-way dedications may be required for renovations exceeding 50% or new construction to provide space for water and sewer lines, stormwater management, sidewalks, and streetlights. She stated that the Comprehensive Plan identifies appropriate right-of-way widths and that older neighborhoods, such as Osceola Park, may require dedications due to smaller lots. She noted that dedications are generally one to three feet and that property owners may request waivers or variances for right-of-way dedications and setbacks.\par
\par
Commissioner Mollica questioned whether property owners willingly dedicate land for right-of-way improvements. Ms. Gelin explained that staff must follow the City\rquote s adopted policies and that property owners may seek waivers or variances. She stated that repeated waiver requests may prompt staff to recommend code adjustments to the Commission and that dedications may also be requested from property owners as neighborhood improvement projects begin.\cf1\kerning0\f0\par
}

