{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Missie Barletto, Public Works Director, reported that parking revenues increased, permit sales more than tripled, parking turnover increased by 12 percent, and citations decreased by nearly 30 percent. She noted that 62 percent of downtown visitors parked for less than an hour and that the first 20 minutes of parking remained free. The Old School Square garage averaged 34 percent occupancy, while Pineapple Grove reached nearly 95 percent occupancy during peak hours. Free Parking Wednesdays increased turnover but slightly decreased occupancy. She also discussed the CRA\rquote s consideration of converting its Northeast Third Avenue parking lot into a parking garage.\par
\par
Mayor Carney supported promoting resident parking permits and stated that parking users should pay for parking. Deputy Vice Mayor Markert supported evaluating additional downtown parking and continuing Free Parking Wednesdays, noting positive feedback from downtown merchants. Commissioner Casale supported continuing Free Parking Wednesdays to help downtown businesses and requested that staff bring the program back for discussion. Commissioners discussed the difference between gross parking revenue and the amount received by the City. Mr. Moore stated that specific revenue-sharing figures would be provided through future budget adjustment processes.\par
\par
Consensus was given by the Commission to direct staff to bring back the continuation of Free Parking Wednesdays for consideration at the November 2 meeting.\par
}

{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mr. Moore provided an update regarding complaints about the beach showers, stating that the City maintains approximately 30 showers and that six were experiencing drainage issues as of the previous day. He reported that maintenance was ongoing and that all showers along the beach and promenade were functioning and being maintained. Commissioner Casale requested more frequent monitoring to address standing water and clogged drains. Mr. Moore stated that he had directed Public Works to address the drainage issues. He also announced a green sea turtle release event scheduled for October 20 at 9:00 a.m. at Atlantic Dunes Park.\par
\par
Mr. Oris provided an update on Subculture, stating that the business needed to amend its zoning certificate of use, submit an updated life safety plan, identify anticipated activities, and obtain a restriping permit for its parking lot. He reported that approximately $100,000 in fines had been levied for parking lot restriping violations and hosting events without proper approvals. He also stated that the Police Department continued issuing citations for illegal parking, while parking on adjacent private property remained a civil matter.\par
\par
Paul Weber, Interim Neighborhood and Community Services Director, stated that Subculture had been ordered to submit a life safety plan within 60 days following a July 23, 2025, special magistrate hearing, but had not complied. He reported that a $250 daily fine was accruing. Commissioner Casale expressed frustration with the continued noncompliance and recommended initiating the lien process. Mayor Carney expressed concern about the lack of a life safety plan. Ms. Gelin clarified that Subculture needed to amend its zoning certificate of use to reflect special events and provide an updated life safety plan to ensure safe ingress and egress.\par
\par
Deputy Vice Mayor Markert expressed concern about potential violence involving neighboring property owners and requested a meeting with Subculture\rquote s management. Darrell Hunter, Police Chief, stated that private property owners were responsible for towing unauthorized vehicles from their lots. Ms. Gelin explained the available code enforcement process, including liens and potential foreclosure proceedings if directed by the Commission. She also noted that property owners could use tow-away signs or chains to prevent unauthorized parking. Vice Mayor Burns requested that staff keep the Commission informed of further developments and enforcement steps.\par
}

