{\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Samuel Camp, Economic Development Manager, presented the resolution. He explained that staff eliminated undersized or oddly configured parcels, rights-of-way, alleyways, medians, underwater properties, properties leased to nonprofits, and properties used for parks, golf courses, administrative buildings, fire stations, lift stations, detention and retention areas, and parking lots. Mr. Camp stated that the list included 20 properties, 13 City-owned and seven CRA-owned. He noted that no Housing Authority or Downtown Development Authority properties were suitable for development under the criteria. He reviewed the properties and explained that some lacked access, were being held for future utility use, were used for parking or storage, fronted unimproved alleyways, or required rezoning and redevelopment. He stated that several CRA properties were under active redevelopment or had development RFPs being prepared. He noted that one property was being considered for higher-use opportunities and that the final City-owned parcel was oddly configured and limited by its location adjacent to I-95.Mr. Camp explained that the statute encouraged municipalities to establish eligibility criteria for developers, make the process for requesting properties publicly available, and ensure long-term affordability through ground leases, right-of-first-refusal, or requiring property reversion if it was not developed.\par
}

