{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Kevin Crowder, BusinessFlare, presented the plan and recommended accepting it as the operating framework for the City\rquote s economic development efforts. He identified three target sectors: founders and creators, boutique personal services and wealth management, and AI-enabled firms and technology-related businesses. He explained that these sectors align with Delray Beach\rquote s brand, identity, and character and rely heavily on attracting talent. Mr. Crowder discussed the need for a more active economic development approach and identified opportunities along Congress Avenue, West Atlantic Avenue, and North Federal Highway. He discussed protecting the Congress Avenue corridor, supporting the CRA\rquote s work on West Atlantic Avenue, and exploring adaptive reuse, wealth management, family offices, and housing opportunities along North Federal Highway. He also emphasized maintaining a healthy mix of housing to support the workforce, creators, and entrepreneurs. Mr. Crowder outlined the first 90 days of implementation, including protecting Congress Avenue, conducting business retention outreach, increasing the visibility of the City\rquote s economic development opportunities, engaging trade publications, and continuing high-touch customer service. The 12-month implementation plan would include updates to the Land Development Regulations (LDR), outreach to financial services and wealth management businesses, identifying real estate opportunities along North Federal Highway, maintaining a property inventory, and regularly monitoring and reporting on economic development activities. Mr. Crowder stated that the plan would be implemented through the City Manager\rquote s Office, working with Samuel Camp, Economic Development Manager and Jeff Oris, Assistant City Manager, and partner organizations. He requested that the Commission accept the plan, affirm the target sectors, and authorize staff to begin the first 90 days of work.\par
\par
Deputy Vice Mayor Markert expressed support for the plan and asked about targeting boutique financial services, private equity, and wealth management firms versus larger companies. Mr. Crowder explained that boutique firms were a better target because they align with Delray Beach\rquote s high-touch, concierge experience and could attract larger firms over time. He stated that the City should build relationships, attend events where targeted businesses are present, and ensure Delray Beach\rquote s story is shared in the appropriate places.\par
}

